# Moving along an axis

Choose {{control:tool.move}} with {{shortcut:tool.move}}, then select one or more objects or
vertices. Press {{shortcut:transform.axis-x}} to lock movement to X,
{{shortcut:transform.axis-y}} for Y, or {{shortcut:transform.axis-z}} for Z. These are the
document's world axes, including when an object itself is rotated.
Press the same key again to remove the axis constraint.
Rotate ({{shortcut:tool.rotate}}) and Scale ({{shortcut:tool.scale}}) also accept axis locks and
[numeric amounts](numeric-transforms.md), using the same axis keys. This guide
focuses on Move dragging and arrow nudges.

While locked, a left-button drag anywhere in {{control:viewport}} moves the
selection along that axis. You can start in empty space; you do not need to
grab a handle. The lock indicator shows the active axis. When the axis points
toward you, the guide says **depth**: drag upward to increase that coordinate,
or downward to decrease it. Movement uses [Auto snapping](snapping.md) by default: the view sets a clean
increment, which stays fixed for that drag.

{{image:axis-locks-drag}}

Use {{shortcut:nudge.left}}, {{shortcut:nudge.right}}, {{shortcut:nudge.up}}, or
{{shortcut:nudge.down}} for **{{value:nudge-step}} cm** nudges. The corresponding
coarse bindings—{{shortcut:nudge.fast-left}}, {{shortcut:nudge.fast-right}},
{{shortcut:nudge.fast-up}}, and {{shortcut:nudge.fast-down}}—request
**{{value:large-nudge-step}} cm**. Changing the [display unit](length-units.md),
zoom, or Auto/Fixed pointer spacing does not change these explicit increments.
With snapping enabled, a nudge aligns an off-grid destination to the
one-centimeter keyboard grid; an on-grid selection moves by the requested amount.
With a lock, arrows follow the
axis as it appears on screen. If the arrow is perpendicular to the visible
axis, or the axis points toward you, {{shortcut:nudge.up}} / {{shortcut:nudge.right}} increase its coordinate and
{{shortcut:nudge.down}} / {{shortcut:nudge.left}} decrease it. For example, with
X locked in Front view, {{shortcut:nudge.right}} or {{shortcut:nudge.up}} moves
in positive X.

Movement stays a **preview** after you release the mouse or arrow key. Continue
with another drag or nudge, or choose another axis; they all belong to the same
Move session. Removing the axis constraint keeps a pending preview and does not
resume background selection. Choose an axis again to continue moving.

Between drags, you can orbit, pan, zoom, or choose a view to inspect the preview.
The axis lock and movement stay in place, and your next drag continues the same
session. Release an active transform drag before starting camera navigation.

Press {{shortcut:edit.confirm}}, or **double-click the viewport**, to apply the whole session as
one undo step and clear the lock. {{shortcut:cancel}} discards every change in that
session and clears the lock while keeping your selection. This works in object
and vertex modes. Finish or cancel before making other edits, changing Local View,
saving, or opening another document. Switching tools cancels the preview.

With no pending session or lock, arrow nudges work in exact aligned views. With the default
Y-up display, Front ({{shortcut:view.front}}) uses
X for left/right and Y for up/down. Right ({{shortcut:view.right}}) uses Z and Y; Top ({{shortcut:view.top}})
uses X and Z. Each arrow follows the direction shown on screen. An oblique
view without a lock leaves the geometry unchanged. See the full
[view-key mapping](view-keys.md) for numpad equivalents.

{{image:axis-locks-aligned-nudge}}

After applying or cancelling, left-drag can draw a selection box again.
Unlocked handle drags and unlocked arrow nudges apply immediately; a completed
drag or separate arrow press is one undo step, with repeats from a held arrow
grouped together. Text fields and open menus keep their keyboard input.
Object moves retain primitive parameters; vertex moves affect only selected
vertices and preserve polygon connections.

See [editing geometry](editing.md) or [selecting objects](object-feedback.md).
[Back to guide](README.md).
